<h2 id="get-started">How to start a side hustle in Indianapolis this month</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Pick one hustle that fits your hours.</strong> Don't start three at once. Choose based on the time you actually have, not the time you wish you had.</li>
  <li><strong>Set your earnings target.</strong> Decide what you need per month and work backward to hours per week. Our <a href="/tools/income-calculator/">income calculator</a> does the math.</li>
  <li><strong>Get listed where people already look.</strong> App sign-ups for gig work; Nextdoor, Facebook neighborhood groups and Google Business Profile for local services.</li>
  <li><strong>Land three clients and ask for reviews.</strong> Early reviews matter more than any ad. Offer a small first-job discount if you need to.</li>
  <li><strong>Mark your event calendar.</strong> Add the 500, Gen Con, the State Fair, the Combine and home-game schedules so you can plan surge weekends.</li>
  <li><strong>Open a separate account and track expenses.</strong> It makes taxes painless and shows you which hustle actually pays.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="indiana-taxes">Indiana taxes and rules for side income</h2>
<p>Indiana keeps things simpler than most states:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> a flat 2.95% for 2026, scheduled to step down further in later years.</li>
  <li><strong>County income tax:</strong> you also pay your county's rate based on where you lived on January 1. Marion County is about 2.02%; Hamilton County is lower.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-employment tax:</strong> 15.3% federal on net profit for Social Security and Medicare, on top of federal income tax. Use the <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a> to estimate your set-aside.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> Indiana's 7% sales tax applies to most physical goods. If you sell products, register for a Retail Merchant Certificate through the state's INTIME portal. Most personal services aren't taxed.</li>
  <li><strong>Licensing:</strong> there's no general statewide business license, and most service hustles in Indianapolis don't need a city license. File an assumed business name if you operate under a brand, and check permits for food sales and regulated trades.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><strong>Quick tip:</strong> Expect a 1099-K or 1099-NEC from apps and clients. Keep receipts for supplies, phone costs and mileage — those deductions lower both income tax and self-employment tax.</div>

